.TH GENERATE_EMOJI_REGEX.PY 1 "September 2026" "mail_filter 1.0.3" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
generate_emoji_regex.py \- generate emoji regular expressions for mail_filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B generate_emoji_regex.py
.BI --download " VERSION"
.RB [ --download-dir " DIR" ]
.RB [ -o " FILE" ]
.br
.B generate_emoji_regex.py
.BI --emoji-data " FILE"
.BI --variation-data " FILE"
.RB [ -o " FILE" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generates an
.I emoji_regex.inc
suitable for a
.B refile:
rule in
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5),
from the Unicode Consortium's own emoji data files.
.PP
The result matches code points whose
.B Emoji_Presentation
property is Yes directly; text\-default symbols that are only emoji when
followed by U+FE0F are matched with that selector required; and keycap
sequences are matched explicitly. It deliberately does not use the broad
.B Emoji=Yes
property, which would classify ordinary ASCII
.BR # ,
.B *
and the digits 0\-9 as emoji and reject a Subject reading
.IR "Invoice #3002" .
.SS Why regenerate it
Unicode adds emoji. A file generated from an older version does not match
the ones added since, so a Subject carrying a new emoji passes a rule
written to catch it \(em silently, because a rule that matches nothing
looks exactly like a rule whose mail has not arrived. The generated file
names the version it came from on its second line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
head -2 /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/emoji_regex.inc
# Generated from official Unicode Emoji 18.0.0 data.
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
Two data files are needed, and there are two ways to obtain them. The two
ways cannot be combined.
.TP
.BI --download " VERSION"
Fetch the two data files from unicode.org:
.PP
.nf
.RS 12
https://www.unicode.org/Public/\fIVERSION\fR/ucd/emoji/emoji-data.txt
https://www.unicode.org/Public/\fIVERSION\fR/ucd/emoji/emoji-variation-sequences.txt
.RE
.fi
.RS 7
.PP
.I VERSION
is a Unicode version such as
.IR 17.0.0 ,
not a \(lqUnicode Emoji\(rq version \(em the path is under
.IR /Public/<Unicode\ version>/ucd/emoji/ .
The two numbering schemes have tracked each other for years, which is why
the distinction is easy to miss.
.PP
A version that has not been published yet does not fail. It redirects to
the draft data, over plain http, and the download follows that without
saying so:
.PP
.nf
https://www.unicode.org/Public/18.0.0/ucd/emoji/emoji-data.txt
  -> 302 -> http://www.unicode.org/Public/draft/ucd/emoji/emoji-data.txt
.fi
.PP
Draft data changes between runs. Check the
.B Date:
line in the header of the downloaded file, which is why
.B --download
keeps what it fetched instead of deleting it.
.RE
.TP
.BI --download-dir " DIR"
Where
.B --download
puts the files it fetched. Default: the current directory. They are kept
rather than deleted, so a later run can reuse them.
.TP
.BI --emoji-data " FILE"
.TQ
.BI --variation-data " FILE"
Use copies already on this machine. Both are required together. This is the
form to use on a host with no route to unicode.org, or where the data files
are held under configuration control so that the generated output can be
reproduced.
.TP
.BI -o ", " --output " FILE"
Write the generated include to
.IR FILE .
Without it the output goes to standard output, which is the form to use
when piping it into
.BR diff (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The include was generated.
.TP
.B 1
A data file could not be downloaded or read.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments do not name a usable pair of data files \(em neither
.B --download
nor both of
.BR --emoji-data " and " --variation-data ,
or both forms at once.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/libexec/mail_filter/generate_emoji_regex.py\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/mail_filter/generate_emoji_regex.py\ \ (Linux)
The installed generator. Deliberately not on
.IR $PATH ;
run it with the full path shown above.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/emoji_regex.inc\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /etc/mail_filter/emoji_regex.inc\ \ (Linux)
The generated include distributed with mail_filter, and where a
regenerated one belongs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate from the current published version:
.nf
.RS
/usr/local/libexec/mail_filter/generate_emoji_regex.py \\
    --download 18.0.0 -o /tmp/emoji_regex.inc
.RE
.fi
.PP
Generate from files fetched earlier, on a host with no outbound access:
.nf
.RS
/usr/local/libexec/mail_filter/generate_emoji_regex.py \\
    --emoji-data emoji-data.txt \\
    --variation-data emoji-variation-sequences.txt \\
    -o /tmp/emoji_regex.inc
.RE
.fi
.SS Installing a regenerated include
Look at what changed before replacing anything \(em the file is what an
active rule matches against, and a mistake here rejects mail:
.PP
.nf
.RS
diff /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/emoji_regex.inc /tmp/emoji_regex.inc
cp /tmp/emoji_regex.inc /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/emoji_regex.inc
mail_filter --configtest && service mail_filter reload
.RE
.fi
.PP
On Linux the directory is
.I /etc/mail_filter
and the reload is
.BR "systemctl reload mail_filter" .
.PP
To see what the new file does to mail you have actually received, before
any of it is in force, run the collected Subject lines against it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
check_regex.py known_good_subjects.txt /tmp/emoji_regex.inc --reverse
.RE
.fi
.PP
which reports the legitimate Subjects the new include would match; see
.BR check_regex.py (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mail_filter (8),
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5),
.BR check_regex.py (1)
